\begin{abstract}
Within SAM3, the Dual-Zero information current on the 12-bridge network defines an action $S_I$ whose stationarity condition selects critical configurations with $\operatorname{Re}(s)=1/2$. This is a geometric variational proposal internal to the model. It is not a proof that every non-trivial zero of the Riemann zeta function lies on the critical line, nor an equivalence between $S_I$ and $\zeta(s)$.
\end{abstract}

\section{What is claimed}
\begin{itemize}
\item A geometrically defined information action $S_I$ built from Dual-Zero regulated spectral data and the 2I bridge structure.
\item Stationarity of $S_I$ forces participating spectral parameters onto the critical line (variational selection principle internal to SAM3).
\end{itemize}

\section{What is not claimed}
\begin{itemize}
\item A proof of the classical Riemann Hypothesis.
\item That every non-trivial zero of $\zeta(s)$ is a critical point of $S_I$.
\item Equivalence of $S_I$ with the Riemann zeta function.
\item Machine-precision identification of SAM3 zeros with the first 100 Riemann zeros as a finished theorem.
\end{itemize}

\section{Status}
The construction is a research direction consistent with the rest of SAM3. Until a complete chain of equalities with controlled remainders is written from the locked spectral action to the classical critical-line statement, RH remains an open residual of the program, not a derived theorem.

Prefer the formulation in Paper 11 for citation.

